#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "624f1134-c016-5bc0-8b8d-60ab4b3ca722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282528} {:answer/id #uuid "a417abba-876d-599b-9802-9bdfb1ffd201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283118} {:answer/id #uuid "b2976f83-d90a-5462-9538-2b4fd5f9d325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283238} {:answer/id #uuid "bd03a3d9-4387-5a6d-83af-e38b3d0f76f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283329} {:answer/id #uuid "d93c61fd-4ae2-5980-8870-7696c25651e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd03a3d9-4387-5a6d-83af-e38b3d0f76f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283329} :answer-field/id #uuid "b37cc56a-bb1a-5c82-a7b5-3e63dd5b826f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "392b472a-8eb5-510e-870b-90564b90ad03" :question/math-academy-id "q-170638" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}]}
